<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Antiochus III the Great</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/222-bce">222</a>–187 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Also known as Antiochus III</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Antiochus III the Great was a Seleucid king who reigned from 222 to 187 BCE. He restored Seleucid power in the east through his anabasis and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coele-syria">Coele-Syria</a> from the Ptolemies. His war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> ended in defeat and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-apamea">Treaty of Apamea</a> in 188 BCE, which imposed a heavy indemnity and began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a>'s decline.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great.jpg" alt="Antiochus III the Great" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carole Raddato from FRANKFURT, Germany · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Male_head_wearing_a_head-band_resembling_king_of_Syria_Antiochus_III_(223%E2%80%93187_BC),_late_1st_century_BC%E2%80%93early_1st_century_AD,_Louvre_Museum_(7462828632).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Antiochus III was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-ii-callinicus">Seleucus II Callinicus</a> and Laodice II, born about 242 BCE near Susa in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>. He succeeded his brother Seleucus III Ceraunus, who was murdered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, while he himself was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>. He was about eighteen and inherited a disorganised state. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> had become detached, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthia">Parthia</a> had broken away. Soon after his accession Media and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persis">Persis</a> revolted under their governors, the brothers Molon and Alexander. An attack on Ptolemaic Syria, made under the influence of his minister Hermeias, ended in failure. Only in 221 BCE, advised by Zeuxis, did Antiochus go east in person, and the rebellion collapsed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After his defeat by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-iv-philopator">Ptolemy IV</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-raphia">Battle of Raphia</a> in 217 BCE, Antiochus turned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> and by 214 BCE had driven his cousin Achaeus, who had taken the royal title, into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a>. He had Achaeus executed. In 209 BCE he invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthia">Parthia</a>, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euthydemus-i">Euthydemus I</a> at the Battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arius">Arius</a>; after the Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkh">Bactra</a> he allowed Euthydemus to keep his royal title. He crossed into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> valley and renewed his friendship with the Indian king Sophagasenus, receiving elephants, and returned by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistan">Seistan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerman">Kerman</a> in 206 or 205 BCE. These campaigns earned him the name the Great, and he took the title Basileus Megas, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-king">Great King</a>.</p><p>Returning west, he renewed the war against the Ptolemies, and his victory over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scopas">Scopas</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-panium">Battle of Panium</a> in 198 BCE marked the end of Ptolemaic rule in Judea. His expansion in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> earned the antagonism of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. In 192 BCE he invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> with 10,000 men, advised by the exiled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> and elected commander of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aetolian-league">Aetolian League</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manius-acilius-glabrio">Manius Acilius Glabrio</a> routed him at Thermopylae in 191 BCE, and Scipio Asiaticus defeated him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manisa">Magnesia ad Sipylum</a> in 190 BCE. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-apamea">Treaty of Apamea</a> in 188 BCE he gave up all lands north and west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taurus-mountains">Taurus Mountains</a>, which Rome gave mostly to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eumenes-ii">Eumenes II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The outlying provinces he had recovered reasserted their independence after Apamea. Antiochus mounted a new expedition to the east and was killed in 187 BCE while pillaging a temple of Bel at Elymaïs. His son Seleucus IV Philopator succeeded him, and later another son became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iv-epiphanes">Antiochus IV Epiphanes</a>. Josephus portrays Antiochus III as friendly to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>: he lowered taxes, granted subventions to the Temple and let the Jews live by the law of their forefathers, and he resettled 2,000 Jewish families from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydia">Lydia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygia">Phrygia</a>. The First Book of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maccabees">Maccabees</a> recalls his defeat by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Antiochus III the Great” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He cooperated with Rome against <b>Antiochus III</b> and rebuilt his kingdom's finances and manpower, but his reign ended in bitterness after he had his younger son Demetrius, a favourite of the Romans, executed in 180 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/antigonid-kingdom-of-macedonia">Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…with Rome, and according to Tacitus, envoys reminded the Senate in 53 CE of the help their city had given to Roman commanders in wars against the Macedonian kings, <b>Antiochus III</b>, Aristonicus, the pirates and Mithridates VI of Pontus; Claudius responded by remitting their tribute for five years.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantium">Byzantium · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the Hellenistic kings Chios remained a nominally free city, carefully balancing between rival dynasties and later cultivating friendship with Rome, which it supported against <b>Antiochus III</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chios">Chios · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Rome's victory over <b>Antiochus III</b> and his Aetolian allies in 191 BCE ended Aetolian control: the consul Manius Acilius Glabrio restored the sanctuary's property and the autonomy of the Delphians, which the Roman Senate soon confirmed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/delphi">Delphi · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After his death at the Battle of Corupedium in 281 BCE the old name returned, and Ephesus passed between the Seleucid Empire and the Ptolemaic Empire until <b>Antiochus III</b> took it in 197–196 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ephesus">Ephesus · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 210 BC <b>Antiochus III</b> attacked Euthydemus, who commanded 10,000 horsemen, lost a battle on the Arius and then held Bactra through a three-year siege until Antiochus recognised him.</blockquote><a href="/empire/greco-bactrian-kingdom">Greco-Bactrian Kingdom · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=32.19,48.26&amp;year=-205" aria-label="Susa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5791 993 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Antiochus III the Great"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6241 1263h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6241 1263h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=32.19,48.26&amp;year=-205">What was here in 205 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
